# A fixture-backed example of starting one exact systemd unit.
#
# This changes system state. Run it only with the disposable unit created by
# scripts/yesh-fixture-test.sh, or inspect it first with:
#
#   ./yesh --plan examples/service-start.yesh
#
# `active` is a systemd manager state, not application readiness. A real
# deployment may need a separate port, socket, HTTP, or domain-specific check.
# The finite budget covers both `systemctl start` and the postcondition poll.
#
# Bash equivalent:
#   systemctl start yesh-codex-fixture.service && \
#     test "$(systemctl show -p ActiveState --value yesh-codex-fixture.service)" = active
# Role of yesh: keep the service or journal request bounded and visible; systemd remains the source of state.

service start yesh-codex-fixture.service until active within 10s
